<!--
  No package attribute. It duplicated the namespace already declared in build.gradle, and
  Android Gradle Plugin 8 rejects it outright:

      Setting the namespace via the package attribute in the source AndroidManifest.xml
      is no longer supported

  A merchant on AGP 8 — which is most of them now — would have had the build fail here. If
  they then ran `adb install -r` out of habit, it reinstalls the PREVIOUS apk, the one built
  before the SDK was swapped in, and everything looks like it worked: install succeeds, the
  app runs, and the receiver is simply not there. That is indistinguishable from "auto-read
  does not work".
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      No SMS permission, deliberately.

      This declared RECEIVE_SMS, and nothing ever used it: auto-read goes through Google's
      SmsRetriever, which reads exactly one message — the one ending in this app's own 11-char
      hash — and needs no permission at all. That is the entire point of the hash.

      Declaring it was not merely redundant. A plugin's manifest is merged into every app that
      depends on it, so every merchant shipped an SMS permission they did not use, and
      RECEIVE_SMS is in Google Play's restricted set: an app declaring it must justify the use
      in a Play Console declaration or be removed from the store. The SDK was creating a review
      problem for its users in exchange for nothing.

      INTERNET is not declared either — Flutter's own manifest already provides it.
    -->

    <!--
      Android 11 (API 30) filters which other apps a package can see, and that filtering
      applies to an explicit broadcast just as it does to a query: setPackage("com.whatsapp")
      on a device where WhatsApp is invisible to us delivers nothing.

      The handshake below is exactly that kind of broadcast, so without this the app targeting
      API 30+ — which is every app now — silently fails to hand WhatsApp the handshake, and
      zero-tap cannot work no matter how correct the template, hash and receiver are. Nothing
      throws; the broadcast is simply dropped.

      Both packages, because the user's code arrives on whichever WhatsApp they have.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.whatsapp" />
        <package android:name="com.whatsapp.w4b" />
    </queries>

    <application>
        <!--
          WhatsApp delivers a zero-tap or one-tap code by broadcasting to the app, not by
          SMS, so SmsRetriever never sees it. Declared statically rather than registered at
          runtime because zero-tap's whole promise is that the user does nothing: the code
          can arrive while the app is backgrounded or not running, and a runtime receiver
          would only exist when the app already does.

          No android:permission, deliberately. It is tempting to restrict the sender, and an
          earlier version of this file guessed at a WhatsApp-owned permission name — which
          would have been the worst possible mistake here, because Android silently refuses
          to deliver a broadcast to a receiver guarded by a permission the sender does not
          hold. The receiver would never fire, nothing would throw, and the symptom would be
          "zero-tap does not work" with no error to chase. Meta's documented receiver
          declares exported="true" and no permission.

          What actually keeps this safe is WhatsApp's own check, not ours: it only broadcasts
          to an app whose package name AND 11-character signing hash match the ones on the
          approved template. An attacker cannot satisfy that without your signing key. The
          receiver additionally rejects anything that is not a plausible code, and passes
          WhatsApp's request_id up to Dart so an app running Meta's handshake can tie the
          code to a request it started.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="io.quickauth.sdk_flutter.WhatsAppOtpReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.whatsapp.otp.OTP_RETRIEVED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
